# The ranked frontier of the single-window atlas

Experiment X-F of `plans/plan-z32-transform.html` (milestone M6; write-up
`plans/note-z32transform-M6.html`, driver `Z32/xf.py`, data
`Z32/data/transform/frontier_autopsy.txt`).

`Z32/README.md` records a frontier for single windows at base `3/2`:

> `L* = 1466/3600 = 0.407222…`, at 14 positions, and nothing at all at `1467/3600`.

That number is a property of the **search mode**, not of the certificate family.  `atlas.c` and
the default `gencert.py` merge the surviving components into interval hulls and then ask for
out-degree `≤ 1`.  The **rank-stratified** criterion — the same `funcOk` relaxation that
`Z32.dubickas_2008_cor_1_2` and `Z32.two_cell_238_empty` already use, sound by the same
`Z32.BlockCert.Cert.not_confined` — certifies that same row at **213** of 236 positions instead of
14, and climbs well past `L*`.  Measured, its certified region in the `(s, L)` plane is a single
tongue narrowing onto the axis `s = (1-L)/2`; that the axis is a symmetry axis is forced, because
the reflection `y ↦ 1 - y` conjugates the carry relation `q y_{n+1} = p y_n - s_n` to itself with
the carry `s ↦ p - q - s` (at `3/2`: `{-1,0,1,2} ↦ {2,1,0,-1}`), so the whole atlas is
symmetric about that axis and the 14 frontier windows fall into 7 mirror pairs.

This file ships the prettiest point of the tongue:

> `[2/7, 5/7]`, of length `3/7 = 0.428571…`, at funnel depth 15 with 166 blocks in 32 rank strata.

## What the entry says, and what it does not

The complement of `[2/7, 5/7]` in `[0,1)` is `{y : ‖y‖ < 2/7}`, so `Z32.two_seven_empty` reads

> for every `ξ ≠ 0` there are infinitely many `n` with `‖ξ(3/2)ⁿ‖ < 2/7 = 0.285714…`

(`Z32.not_forall_two_seven_le_abs_sub_round`).  The corpus's previous best of this shape was
`1/3`, from the length-`1/3` line (`Z32.ZSet_three_two_third_empty` at the centred window
`[1/3, 2/3)`); the longest single window in print is [Dub19] Theorem 1.2's `31/81 = 0.38271…`,
which the corpus already passes at `0.40722`.

It does **not** beat print in the nearest-integer coordinate.  [Dub06JNT] Corollary 1 (Dubickas,
J. Number Theory 117 (2006) 222-239, p. 225; also Theorem 3.14 of Bugeaud's Cambridge Tract 193)
gives every `ξ ≠ 0` a limit point of `(‖ξ(3/2)ⁿ‖)` at most `(1 + T(2/3))/4 = 0.285647…`, which is
smaller than `2/7` by `6.7·10⁻⁵` — and, by the cascade below, no entry of this family ever will.
This is the exact mirror of experiment X-238, whose shipped entry `c = 0.238` sits `1.2·10⁻⁴` below
the *same paper's* other constant `0.238117558…` (`Z32.two_cell_238_empty`), with its measured
ceiling within `2·10⁻⁷` of it.  So the block certificate reaches both of [Dub06JNT]'s constants to
five decimal places and passes neither — and neither of that paper's constants is formalised, while
both of the engine's are.

## Where the tongue really ends: a Thue-Morse cascade

`3/7` is not the tip.  On the centred line write the window as `[c, 1-c)`; an orbit lies inside
exactly when `c ≤ min_i ‖y_i‖`, and the orbits enter, as `c` falls, in a period-doubling cascade
whose carry words are the **Thue-Morse prefixes** (up to a cyclic shift) and whose entry points
have the closed form

> `4 c_k - 1 = 3 · ∏_{n=1}^{k-2} (3^{2ⁿ} - 2^{2ⁿ}) / (3^{2^{k-1}} + 2^{2^{k-1}})`,
> `c₁ = 2/5`, `c₂ = 4/13`, `c₃ = 28/97`, `c₄ = 1948/6817`, `c₅ = 12314908/43112257`, …

so that `c_k ↓ (1 + T(2/3))/4 = 0.285647324744458851…`, where `T(z) = ∏_{n≥0}(1 - z^{2ⁿ})`.  That
limit is *exactly* [Dub06JNT] Corollary 1's small limit point, and below it the centred window
contains every `c_k`, hence infinitely many periodic orbits, which
`Z32.BlockCert.Cert.ok_eq_false_of_infinite_cycles` forbids.  Measured, the family certifies at
`c = 0.2856475` (`1.8·10⁻⁷` above the constant) and fails at `c = 0.2856472`, so the constant lies
in the bracket.  Symmetrically,
experiment X-238's two-cell ceiling is the same cascade read from the other side, converging to
`(3 - T(2/3))/12 = 0.238117558…`, [Dub06JNT]'s large limit point, which X-238 measured in
`(0.2381175, 0.2381177]`.

**So this certificate family can never beat [Dub06JNT] on either side** — not for want of search,
but because the printed constants are the accumulation points of its own obstruction.  `2/7` sits
`6.7·10⁻⁵` above the first of them.

## What cuts the frontier row

Not a shortage of cycles.  Experiment X-F ran the survivor-cycle inventory to period 20 over the
whole `L*` row: the 14 certified windows contain exactly one periodic orbit, the 2-cycle
`{2/5, 3/5}` — which is forced, since `Z32.BlockCert.Cert.eq_of_memI_block` caps the hold set of a
2-block certificate at 2 points — but so do the two positions that bracket them, `960/3600` and
`1174/3600`, and those are refused at every depth in every mode.  Conjecture C-6 ("the frontier
windows are *precisely* those whose only orbit is the 2-cycle") is therefore half true: the
forward implication is that theorem, and the converse fails.

What separates them is the transient tree, and the mechanism is the one experiment X-D19 already
identified in [Dub19]'s window: `4/15 ↦ 2/5` and `11/15 ↦ 3/5` under `y ↦ (3y - s)/2`, so a window
containing `4/15` traps an infinite backward chain accumulating on the 2-cycle, the alive component
count is exactly `k+1` at depth `k`, and no finite interval partition ever separates the chain from
the cycle.  Since `4/15 = 960/3600` and `11/15 = 2640/3600`, the ranked-certified set of the row is
*exactly* `961 ≤ i ≤ 1173` — the windows whose closure lies in `(4/15, 11/15)`.  Emptiness itself is
not in doubt at `960/3600`: every confined orbit there is eventually the 2-cycle, hence has an
eventually periodic carry word, which `Z32.not_isEventuallyPeriodic_carry` forbids.  The
certificate format, not the mathematics, is what runs out.
-/

set_option maxRecDepth 4000000
set_option maxHeartbeats 4000000

namespace Z32.BlockCert

